% ============================================================================
% muscle-striation — for each of the three muscle-tissue types, whether it
% is striated or not, as the SAME NCI SEER spans also state
% (adj-facts-stdlib, BIOLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `muscle-types.adj`
% (`muscle_trait(muscle, trait)`, ONE distinctive characteristic per muscle
% type — skeletal → voluntary, smooth → involuntary, cardiac →
% intercalated_disks). That table's own header already quotes, verbatim,
% each muscle type's defining SEER sentence, and buried in every one of
% those three spans, alongside the trait the parent table already captures,
% is a second, cleanly binary fact the voluntary/trait schema had no room
% for: whether the tissue is STRIATED or not.
%
%   skeletal → striated (yes)
%     "Skeletal muscle fibers are cylindrical, multinucleated, striated,
%      and under voluntary control."
%
%   smooth → not striated (no)
%     "Smooth muscle cells are spindle shaped, have a single, centrally
%      located nucleus, and lack striations. They are called involuntary
%      muscles."
%
%   cardiac → striated (yes)
%     "Cardiac muscle has branching fibers, one nucleus per cell,
%      striations, and intercalated disks."
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? muscle_striated(cardiac, $Striated)   % yes
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, an ordinary two-column EXACT
% lookup): each `row` lowers to a ground relation `muscle_striated(muscle,
% striated)` carrying the citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD
% binding query — the engine returns the striation fact WITH its source, on
% the CPU, with zero model calls. All three muscle types `muscle-types.adj`
% ships have a striation fact stated in this SAME cited span, so this
% sibling covers the full three-type domain with no abstention. The query
% also runs BACKWARD — bind `no` and recall the one non-striated type:
%
%     ? muscle_striated($Muscle, no)
%       % smooth
%
% Provenance: the SEER Training Modules "Muscle Tissue" page. This paragraph
% used to say the spans were reproduced "byte-for-byte" with "no new
% WebFetch"; they are now measured against the page itself.
%
% EACH ROW CARRIES ITS OWN SENTENCE (ADJ-TABLES RS-5e, #14986). The envelope
% used to be the skeletal sentence, the primary source of all three answers,
% with the smooth and cardiac sentences only table-level corroborations. Each
% of the three sentences names its muscle type and whether it is striated, so
% every row now takes its own as a `source`. The smooth row takes only the
% first of the two sentences quoted above: the second ("They are called
% involuntary muscles.") names no striation. The envelope is now the page's
% framing sentence, which names no muscle type and no striation:
%
%     "Muscle tissue is composed of cells that have the special ability to
%      shorten or contract in order to produce movement of the body parts."
%
% Measured 2026-09-15 on the raw page (HTTP 200; a nonsense path on the same
% host returns 404), with inline tags removed without a space and only ASCII
% whitespace collapsed: the three row sentences and the envelope each occur
% exactly once, each inside a `<p>`. Every row's page is the envelope's, so no
% row restates a `locator` line or `trust` (ADJ-TABLES §4). `trust
% authoritative` — U.S. National Cancer Institute, a primary U.S. government
% teaching resource.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table muscle_striated {
    columns muscle, striated

    row (skeletal, yes) {
        source "Skeletal muscle fibers are cylindrical, multinucleated, striated, and under voluntary control."
    }
    row (smooth, no) {
        source "Smooth muscle cells are spindle shaped, have a single, centrally located nucleus, and lack striations."
    }
    row (cardiac, yes) {
        source "Cardiac muscle has branching fibers, one nucleus per cell, striations, and intercalated disks."
    }

    % THE ENVELOPE WAS THE SKELETAL ROW'S OWN SENTENCE, the primary source of
    % all three answers, with the smooth and cardiac sentences only table-level
    % corroborations. It is now a framing sentence that names no muscle type
    % and no striation. Every row overrides it, so it reaches no answer.
    source "Muscle tissue is composed of cells that have the special ability to shorten or contract in order to produce movement of the body parts."
    locator "https://training.seer.cancer.gov/anatomy/cells_tissues_membranes/tissues/muscle.html"
    trust authoritative
}
